      "id": 1,
      "text": "Tyro sprang up in the mid‑1800s as a crossroads village, its name honoring Joseph H. Thompson’s Iron Works and general store.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/tyro_north_carolina",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The Tyro Tavern, a Greek‑Revival brick inn built around 1840, is the only surviving building from Thompson’s mercantile empire and is on the National Register.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        7
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/tyro_north_carolina",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Just six miles west, Boone’s Cave Park on the Yadkin River hosts a legend that Daniel Boone once hid there from pursuing Native Americans.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        18,
        19
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tyro%2C_North_Carolina",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Tyro sits in central North Carolina, nestled between the Appalachian Mountains to the west and the Mid‑Atlantic coast to the east.",
